      "text": "\n\n\nDon\u0027t worry about those `dompurify` warnings! You can safely ignore them for this project.\n\n**Here is why:**\n`monaco-editor` uses the `dompurify` library to safely display formatted text (like the little pop-up tooltips you see when you hover over code). The warning is for an \"XSS\" (Cross-Site Scripting) vulnerability, which is only dangerous if you are building a website where *other* strangers can upload malicious code to your screen. \n\nSince you are building a standalone simulator where *you* (or the student) are the only one typing the Arduino C++ code, there is zero risk of a hacker injecting malicious tooltips into your local editor. `npm audit fix` couldn\u0027t automatically fix it because Microsoft (who makes Monaco) needs to update their specific dependency tree, but it will not affect our app\u0027s performance or security at all.\n\n### Let\u0027s keep the momentum going!\n\nSince all the packages are successfully installed, you are ready to fire up the interface. \n\nRun this command in your terminal:\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n1. Open the local link it gives you (usually `http://localhost:5173`).\n2. You should see the dark-themed \"Flawless Simulator\" UI with the code editor on the left and the simulation/control panels on the right.\n\nDoes the UI load up successfully on your screen? Once you confirm it looks good, we will start writing the engine that takes your C++ code and compiles it into machine language!",
      "role": "model",
      "finishReason": "STOP",
      "tokenCount": 331,
      "parts": [{
        "text": "\n\n\nDon\u0027t worry about those `dompurify` warnings! You can safely ignore them for this project.\n\n**"
      }, {
        "text": "Here is why:**\n`monaco-editor` uses the `dompurify` library to safely display formatted text (like the"
      }, {
        "text": " little pop-up tooltips you see when you hover over code). The warning is for an \"XSS\" (Cross"
      }, {
        "text": "-Site Scripting) vulnerability, which is only dangerous if you are building a website where *other* strangers can upload"
      }, {
        "text": " malicious code to your screen. \n\nSince you are building a standalone simulator where *you* (or the student) are the only"
      }, {
        "text": " one typing the Arduino C++ code, there is zero risk of a hacker injecting malicious tooltips into your local editor."
