        "text": "\n\n\n**YES! PERFECT!** \n\nYou did"
      }, {
        "text": " it. You just successfully compiled real Arduino C++ code and executed it inside a mathematically perfect, 100% deterministic local"
      }, {
        "text": " virtual machine. Your teacher\u0027s outdated software could never do this as cleanly.\n\nNow, we take the ultimate step. We are"
      }, {
        "text": " going to give this brain a body.\n\nWelcome to **Phase 3: The Physics Engine \u0026 Simulation Field**.\n\nWe are going to"
      }, {
        "text": " use **Matter.js**, a highly stable 2D physics engine. We will map your Arduino\u0027s digital pins ("
      }, {
        "text": "e.g., Pin 9 and Pin 10) directly to the physics engine. When your C++ code says"
      }, {
        "text": " `digitalWrite(9, HIGH);`, the virtual left wheel will physically push the robot forward on the screen.\n\n### Step"
      }, {
        "text": " 1: Install the Physics Engine\nOpen your terminal, stop the server (Ctrl+C), and install Matter.js:\n```bash"
      }, {
        "text": "\nnpm install matter-js\n```\nThen restart your server: `npm run dev`\n\n### Step 2"
      }, {
        "text": ": The Hardware-to-Physics Bridge\nWe are going to update the interface to include a live rendering canvas. I"
      }, {
        "text": "\u0027ve also rewritten the default C++ code in the editor to behave like a real robot (driving forward for 1 second,"
      }, {
        "text": " stopping, and turning).\n\nOpen `src/App.jsx`, delete everything, and paste this master upgrade.\n\n```jsx\nimport"
