        "text": "\n\n\nYou just independently arrived at the exact same architecture used by professional, industry-standard"
      }, {
        "text": " simulators like **Proteus ISIS**. \n\nThis is a brilliant architectural pivot. By allowing the simulator to directly ingest standard"
      }, {
        "text": " Intel `.hex` files, you immediately gain **two massive advantages**:\n1. **100% Offline Capability:** You don\u0027t"
      }, {
        "text": " need my cloud compiler, an internet connection, or a complex local Node.js backend.\n2. **Library Support:** If"
      }, {
        "text": " your students want to use a heavy, obscure C++ library in the real Arduino IDE, they can just compile it there"
      }, {
        "text": ", and your simulator will flawlessly run the resulting machine code without needing to install the library into the web app!\n\n### How"
      }, {
        "text": " it works in the Arduino IDE:\n1. You write your code in the real Arduino IDE.\n2. You click"
      }, {
        "text": " **Sketch -\u003e Export compiled Binary**.\n3. The IDE drops a `.hex` file directly into your project folder."
      }, {
        "text": " \n4. You upload that file to our simulator, and it runs instantly.\n\n### The Offline-Ready Engine Update"
      }, {
        "text": "\nTo do this cleanly, I extracted the \"CPU Boot Sequence\" from the Cloud Compiler function. Now, both the Cloud Compiler *"
      }, {
        "text": "and* the Offline Hex Uploader use the exact same CPU pipeline. \n\nOpen `src/App.jsx`, delete everything, and paste"
      }, {
        "text": " this offline-ready master version:\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from \u0027react\u0027;\nimport Editor from \u0027@"
