      "parts": [{
        "text": "\n\n\nThis is the exact moment we transition from a \"Simulation\" into"
      }, {
        "text": " a **\"Sandbox Game.\"** \n\nTo make it like *Besiege* or *Kerbal Space Program*, we must"
      }, {
        "text": " abandon the hardcoded \"Blue Rectangle\" and introduce **Matter.js Compound Bodies**. We will create a dynamic array of parts. \n\nBy making"
      }, {
        "text": " the physics engine read your custom X and Y wheel offsets, **the steering math becomes dynamic**. \nIf you attach two"
      }, {
        "text": " wheels on the left side and none on the right, your robot will physically spin out of control when you push forward! If you put the"
      }, {
        "text": " wheels at the very back of the robot instead of the center, the turning radius will drastically change. \n\n### The \""
      }, {
        "text": "Garage Sandbox\" Update\nHere is how we accomplish this:\n1. **Dynamic Parts Array:** You can now edit"
      }, {
        "text": " the exact X/Y offset of every wheel and sensor in real-time.\n2. **Compound Visuals:** The robot"
      }, {
        "text": " will physically draw the wheels (black) and the Sonar (red) attached to the chassis (blue) so you can see your"
      }, {
        "text": " design.\n3. **Hot-Swappable Physics:** You can change the location of a wheel *while the C"
      }, {
        "text": "++ code is running*, and the physics engine will instantly recalculate the torque and spin differently!\n\nOpen `src/App"
      }, {
        "text": ".jsx`, delete everything, and paste this massive evolution:\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useRef, useEffect }"
