        "text": "This is the exact"
      }, {
        "text": " mindset needed to take this from a \"toy\" to a **professional engineering tool**. Hardcoding limits creativity. If a user wants to build a 6"
      }, {
        "text": "-wheel-drive rover, a 1-wheel spinner, or a robot with 4 sonar sensors facing different directions, the"
      }, {
        "text": " software should absolutely let them.\n\nWe are going to implement a **Fully Dynamic Component Pipeline**. \n\n### What this"
      }, {
        "text": " update does:\n1. **The Component Factory:** Added an \"Add Component\" menu. You can spawn as many Motors"
      }, {
        "text": " or Sonar sensors as you want.\n2. **Deletable Parts:** Every component now has a \"Trash"
      }, {
        "text": "\" icon. Don\u0027t want a left motor? Delete it. The physics engine will instantly redraw the robot without it.\n3."
      }, {
        "text": " **Dynamic Kinematics Engine:** I completely ripped out the hardcoded `pin9` and `pin10` math"
      }, {
        "text": ". The physics engine now loops through *every* part you created. If it finds 6 wheels, it applies 6 physical"
      }, {
        "text": " force vectors based on their exact X/Y offsets and Pin states.\n4. **Cleaned up the HUD:** Because"
      }, {
        "text": " parts are infinite, I moved the \"ON/OFF\" indicators out of the HUD and directly onto the components list. When"
      }, {
        "text": " a pin goes `HIGH`, that specific part in your list will glow green.\n\nOpen `src/App.jsx"
      }, {
        "text": "`, delete everything, and paste this ultimate Sandbox evolution:\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from"
      }, {
