        "text": "[]` in `DEFAULT_CONFIG`. I\u0027ve added state and refs for arena objects, set up a `useEffect` for config syncing with `rebuildArenaPhysics`, and updated the engine setup, ensuring it won\u0027t remove walls.\n\n\n**Refining Simulation Reset**\n\nI\u0027m now refining the `resetSimulation` function to reset arena object positions. I will iterate through `initConfig.arenaObjects` to ensure objects return to their initial positions. This will help maintain a consistent simulation state upon reset. I also updated `rebuildArenaPhysics` to visualize static and dynamic objects with distinct colors.\n\n\n**Finalizing Tabbed UI**\n\nI\u0027m now finalizing the HTML and CSS for the tabbed interface. I\u0027ve got the basic layout working, and I\u0027m adding styling. This includes the implementation of robust state management and a basic arena tab layout. I\u0027ve got a \"Robot\" and \"Arena\" tab, which now contain the appropriate HTML and CSS. I\u0027m focusing on user-friendliness and visual clarity in the tabbed interface. I will be incorporating import/export buttons.\n\n\n**Implementing Object Removal**\n\nI\u0027m now implementing the object removal function within the arena objects list. I\u0027ve added a button that triggers `removeArenaObject` and successfully implemented the object removal and UI updates. I can now remove objects from the arena directly through the tabbed interface.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Object Properties**\n\nI\u0027m now synchronizing object properties with the HUD. I\u0027m updating x, y, and angle values directly in the DOM using IDs, ensuring the inputs are updated after updates, but not when the input is active. This avoids React renders. This is being done inside `afterUpdate`, along with other properties.\n\n\n**Validating Configuration Updates**\n\nI\u0027m now focusing on a few key edge cases, especially related to configuration and object management. Restoring defaults should clear arena objects. I\u0027ve updated the `DEFAULT_CONFIG` by adding two default blocks as examples. I\u0027m also ensuring that the `arenaBodiesRef` always stays updated to avoid issues in the `afterUpdate` loop.\n\n\nThis is an absolutely incredible idea. You don\u0027t just want a robot testing ground; you want a **Level Editor**! \n\nBy adding the ability to spawn custom physical props, you can build a labyrinth, a parking garage, or place loose physics boxes to test your robot\u0027s pushing strength or sonar collision detection!\n\n### What this update does:\n1. **The Workshop Tabs:** I upgraded the \"Robot Customization Lab\" into a tabbed interface. You now have **"
      }, {
        "text": "[ ROBOT ]** and **[ ARENA ]** tabs.\n2. **The Level Editor:** Inside the ARENA tab, you can spawn"
      }, {
        "text": " \"World Objects\". You can define their Width, Height, X, Y, and Angle.\n3. **The"
      }, {
        "text": " \"Fixed Wall\" Toggle:** Every object has a checkbox. \n   * If **checked (Gray)**: It is bolted"
      }, {
        "text": " to the ground. It acts as an immovable brick wall for mazes. \n   * If **unchecked (Purple)**: It is a"
      }, {
        "text": " dynamic physics prop (like a wooden crate). Your robot can crash into it and physically push it out of the way!\n4. **"
      }, {
        "text": "Drag \u0026 Drop Editing:** Just like the robot, you can grab these crates and walls with your mouse and drag them around"
      }, {
        "text": " the canvas. The UI will dynamically track their coordinates in real-time.\n\nOpen `src/App.jsx`, select"
      }, {
